No trouble at all. Add a negative lookahead at the start:

```javascript
const ZIP_RE = /^(?!00000)[0-9]{5}(-[0-9]{4})?$/;
```

- `(?!00000)` fails the match if the string starts with `00000`, and it doesn't consume any characters.
- The rest is unchanged, so `00000-1234` is rejected too.

Add the cases to the test file:

```javascript
test("rejects all-zero ZIP", () => {
  assert.ok(!ZIP_RE.test("00000"));
  assert.ok(!ZIP_RE.test("00000-6789"));
});

test("still accepts ZIPs with leading zeros", () => {
  assert.ok(ZIP_RE.test("00501"));      // Holtsville, NY
  assert.ok(ZIP_RE.test("02134-1234"));
});
```

If you also want to reject an all-zero +4 (`12345-0000`), which USPS doesn't assign either:

```javascript
const ZIP_RE = /^(?!00000)[0-9]{5}(-(?!0000)[0-9]{4})?$/;
```

Lookaheads work the same in Python, so you can use these patterns there unchanged.

I'd go with **`isValidZipCode`**. It's clear and reads naturally in conditionals:

```javascript
const ZIP_RE = /^(?!00000)[0-9]{5}(-(?!0000)[0-9]{4})?$/;

function isValidZipCode(input) {
  return ZIP_RE.test(input);
}

if (!isValidZipCode(form.zip)) { /* show error */ }
```

