The original `+` was doing double duty, so here's the version that commits to numbers:

```typescript
function add(a: number, b: number): number {
  return a + b;
}
```

The JavaScript version also concatenated strings (`add("1", 2)` → `"12"`), which is exactly the kind of surprise TypeScript exists to stop. If you need string support too, use overloads so each call keeps a precise return type:

```typescript
function add(a: number, b: number): number;
function add(a: string, b: string): string;
function add(a: any, b: any): any {
  return a + b;
}
```

For plain arithmetic, the first one is the right call.

Floating point is the only real trap in a function this small, so the test handles it:

```typescript
// add.test.ts
import { add } from "./add";

describe("add", () => {
  it("adds two positive numbers", () => {
    expect(add(2, 3)).toBe(5);
  });

  it("handles negative numbers", () => {
    expect(add(-2, -3)).toBe(-5);
    expect(add(-2, 3)).toBe(1);
  });

  it("treats zero as the identity", () => {
    expect(add(0, 7)).toBe(7);
    expect(add(7, 0)).toBe(7);
  });

  it("adds decimals within floating-point precision", () => {
    // 0.1 + 0.2 === 0.30000000000000004, so toBe(0.3) would fail
    expect(add(0.1, 0.2)).toBeCloseTo(0.3);
  });

  it("propagates NaN and Infinity", () => {
    expect(add(NaN, 1)).toBeNaN();
    expect(add(Infinity, 1)).toBe(Infinity);
  });

  it("rejects non-number arguments at compile time", () => {
    // @ts-expect-error: strings aren't allowed by the signature
    add("1", 2);
  });
});
```

For this to run:

1. **Export the function** in `add.ts`:
   ```typescript
   export function add(a: number, b: number): number {
     return a + b;
   }
   ```
2. **Let Jest read TypeScript.** The quickest option is `ts-jest`:
   ```bash
   npm install --save-dev jest ts-jest @types/jest
   npx ts-jest config:init
   npx jest
   ```

The `@ts-expect-error` test only does its job if type-checking runs. `ts-jest` checks types by default. If you use Babel or SWC, add a separate `tsc --noEmit` step. Otherwise that test passes no matter what the types say.

If you're using the overloaded string/number version, add a `expect(add("a", "b")).toBe("ab")` case and drop the compile-time rejection test.

